 "lessons": {
  "L1": {
   "id": "L1",
   "n": 1,
   "title": "Noun gender and the prepositional case (where?)",
   "goal": "By the end you'll be able to say where you are and where things are: Я в храме. Я на вокзале. Мы в Беларуси.",
   "minutes": "15–20 min to read, then 31 exercises",
   "sections": [
    {
     "h": "Step 1 · Every noun has a gender",
     "open": true,
     "html": "<p>Every Russian noun is <b>masculine</b>, <b>feminine</b> or <b>neuter</b>. It's grammar, not meaning: a church can be masculine (<span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">храм</span>) or feminine (<span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">церковь</span>). The good news: the <b>last letter of the dictionary form</b> almost always tells you.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>Ends in</th><th>Gender</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a consonant</td><td>masculine (он)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">магазин</span> store · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">храм</span> church · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">паспорт</span> passport · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Минск</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-й</span></td><td>masculine (он)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">музей</span> museum · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">чай</span> tea · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">трамвай</span> tram</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-а</span></td><td>feminine (она)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">касса</span> checkout · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">улица</span> street · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">граница</span> border · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">работа</span> work</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-я</span></td><td>feminine (она)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">таможня</span> customs · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">деревня</span> village · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Россия</span> Russia</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-о</span></td><td>neuter (оно)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">окно</span> window · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">место</span> place, seat · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">молоко</span> milk</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td>neuter (оно)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">море</span> sea · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">здание</span> building · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">расписание</span> timetable</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ь</span></td><td>either</td><td>see Step 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"tip\"><b>One exception to know now:</b> men are masculine even when the word ends in <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-а/-я</span>: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">папа</span> (dad), <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">дедушка</span> (grandpa), <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">батюшка</span> (Father, the priest), <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">мужчина</span> (man).</p><p class=\"tip\"><b>Habit:</b> learn every noun with он, она or оно: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">магазин — он, касса — она, окно — оно</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
     "h": "Step 2 · Nouns ending in -ь",
     "open": false,
     "html": "<p>The soft sign <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">ь</span> doesn't tell you the gender by itself. A few clues help:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>Ending</th><th>Gender</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-чь, -шь, -щь, -жь</span></td><td>always feminine</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">ночь</span> night · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">дочь</span> daughter · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">вещь</span> thing (masculine words with these sounds have no <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">ь</span>: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">врач</span> doctor, <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">нож</span> knife)</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ость</span></td><td>feminine</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">новость</span> news · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">радость</span> joy</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-тель</span></td><td>masculine</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">учитель</span> teacher · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">водитель</span> driver · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">житель</span> resident</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For everything else, just learn it with он or она. Common ones:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>Masculine (он)</th><th>Feminine (она)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">рубль</span> ruble</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">церковь</span> church</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">день</span> day</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">очередь</span> line, queue</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Гомель</span> (the city)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Беларусь</span> Belarus</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">площадь</span> square · <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">дверь</span> door</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
    },
    {
     "h": "Step 3 · What the prepositional case is for",
     "open": false,
     "html": "<p>Russian nouns change their ending depending on their job in the sentence. These forms are called <b>cases</b>. This lesson is about the <b>prepositional case</b> (<span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">предложный падеж</span>). It gets its name because it's only used after a preposition.</p><p>After <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в</span> (in) or <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на</span> (on, at), it answers the question <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">где?</span> (where?):</p><ul class=\"exl\"><li><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Где батюшка? — Батюшка в храме.</span><span class=\"g\">Where's Father? — Father is in the church.</span></li><li><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Где ты? — Я на вокзале.</span><span class=\"g\">Where are you? — I'm at the train station.</span></li><li><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Где вы живёте? — Я живу в Минске.</span><span class=\"g\">Where do you live? — I live in Minsk.</span></li></ul><p class=\"tip\">Russian has no \"is / am / are\" in the present tense. <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Я в храме</span> is simply “I (am) in the church.”</p><p>The same endings are used after <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">о</span> (about): <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Я думаю о Минске</span> (I'm thinking about Minsk).</p>"
    },
    {
     "h": "Step 4 · The endings: -е or -и",
     "open": false,
     "html": "<p>Take the dictionary form, look at the ending, and apply one of two rules.</p><h4>Rule 1 · Most nouns take -е</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>Dictionary form</th><th>What happens</th><th>Где?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">храм</span></td><td>add <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в храме</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Минск</span></td><td>add <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Минске</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">магазин</span></td><td>add <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в магазине</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">музей</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-й</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в музее</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">работа</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-а</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на работе</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">граница</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-а</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на границе</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">деревня</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-я</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в деревне</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">таможня</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-я</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на таможне</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">место</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-о</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на месте</span> (in place, on the spot)</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">море</span></td><td>stays <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на море</span> (at the seaside)</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Гомель</span> (masc.)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ь</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Гомеле</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Rule 2 · Two groups take -и</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Dictionary form</th><th>Где?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>feminine <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ь</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">церковь</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в церкви</span> (the о drops out)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Беларусь</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Беларуси</span></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">очередь</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в очереди</span> (in line)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">площадь</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на площади</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ия</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Россия</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в России</span></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">литургия</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на литургии</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ие</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">здание</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в здании</span></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">расписание</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в расписании</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ий</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">санаторий</span> (health resort)</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в санатории</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"tip\"><b>Memory hook:</b> feminine <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ь</span>, and anything with <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">и</span> just before the ending (<span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ия, -ие, -ий</span>), takes <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-и</span>. Everything else takes <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span>. So it's <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в России</span>, never “в Россие”, and <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Гомеле</span> (masculine), but <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Беларуси</span> (feminine).</p><h4>Around Belarus</h4><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>City / country</th><th>Где?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Минск</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Минске</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Брест</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Бресте</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Витебск</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Витебске</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Могилёв</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Могилёве</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Гомель</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Гомеле</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Березино</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Березине</span> (in everyday speech you'll also hear <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Березино</span>)</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Беларусь</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Беларуси</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
    },
    {
     "h": "Step 5 · в or на?",
     "open": false,
     "html": "<p><b><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в</span> = in, inside.</b> Use it for buildings and rooms, and for cities and countries: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в храме, в церкви, в магазине, в Минске, в Беларуси</span>.</p><p><b><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на</span> = on, at.</b> Use it for surfaces (<span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на столе</span>, on the table), for events and activities (<span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на литургии, на службе, на работе</span>), and for a set of places you just have to learn:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Где?</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">рынок</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на рынке</span></td><td>at the market (the о drops out)</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">вокзал</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на вокзале</span></td><td>at the train station</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">работа</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на работе</span></td><td>at work</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">почта</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на почте</span></td><td>at the post office</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">улица</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на улице</span></td><td>in the street; outside</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">граница</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на границе</span></td><td>at the border</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">касса</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на кассе</span></td><td>at the checkout (for a ticket office you'll also hear <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в кассе</span>)</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">остановка</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на остановке</span></td><td>at the (bus) stop</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">таможня</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на таможне</span></td><td>at customs</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">площадь</span></td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на площади</span></td><td>in the square</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"tip\"><b>Habit:</b> learn new place words together with their preposition: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в магазине, на вокзале</span>. <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на улице</span> also just means “outside”: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">На улице холодно</span> (It's cold outside).</p>"
    },
    {
     "h": "Step 6 · во and об",
     "open": false,
     "html": "<p>Two small spelling helpers make words easier to say:</p><p><b><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в → во</span></b> before a word starting with <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в</span> or <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">ф</span> plus another consonant: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">во Франции</span> (in France), <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">во Владимире</span> (in Vladimir). But: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в Минске, в Витебске</span> (a vowel follows the в).</p><p><b><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">о → об</span></b> (about) before a vowel sound (<span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">а, о, у, э, и</span>): <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Я думаю об Америке</span> (I'm thinking about America). But: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">о Минске</span>.</p><p><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на</span> never changes.</p>"
    },
    {
     "h": "Step 7 · A few exceptions",
     "open": false,
     "html": "<p>A small group of masculine nouns take a stressed <b><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-у</span></b> after <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в / на</span> when they mean location. Learn these as set phrases:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"lt \"><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Где?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">аэропорт</span> airport</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в аэропорту</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">лес</span> forest</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в лесу</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">мост</span> bridge</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на мосту</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">сад</span> garden</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в саду</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">пол</span> floor</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на полу</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">шкаф</span> wardrobe</td><td><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в шкафу</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>After <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">о</span> (about) they're regular: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">о лесе, об аэропорте</span>. You'll hear <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в аэропорте</span> in casual speech, but <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в аэропорту</span> is the standard form.</p><p>Also good to know: <b>at home</b> is <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">дома</span> (not “в доме”). Foreign words like <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">кафе, метро, такси</span> never change: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в кафе, в метро, в такси</span>.</p>"
    },
    {
     "h": "Summary",
     "open": false,
     "html": "<ul class=\"sum\"><li>Gender: consonant or <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-й</span> → masculine; <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-а / -я</span> → feminine; <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-о / -е</span> → neuter; <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ь</span> → learn it. Men are masculine.</li><li><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">Где?</span> = <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в / на</span> + prepositional.</li><li>Endings: <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-е</span>, except feminine <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ь</span> and <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-ия / -ие / -ий</span> → <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">-и</span>.</li><li><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">на</span> for events and for <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">рынок, вокзал, работа, почта, улица, граница, касса, остановка</span>.</li><li><span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">во / об</span> before awkward clusters and vowels; <span lang=\"ru\" class=\"r\">в аэропорту, в лесу, на мосту</span>.</li></ul>"
    }
   ],
   "parts": {
    "A": "Part A · Pick the gender",
    "B": "Part B · Form the prepositional",
    "C": "Part C · в or на + the right form"
   },
   "practice": {
    "title": "Now use it: write to your tutor",
    "text": "Write 3 short sentences to your tutor in chat saying where people or things are. Use at least one на-word and one -и ending.",
    "examples": [
     "Я живу в Минске.",
     "Батюшка в церкви.",
     "Мой паспорт на столе."
    ]
   },
